WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:07.770
Dark chocolate.

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:08.900
White chocolate.

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:09.733
Biscuits.

00:00:09.733 --> 00:00:10.888
Hot chocolate.

00:00:10.888 --> 00:00:14.310
Pralines, bonbons, chocolate
cake, chocolate bars...

00:00:15.350 --> 00:00:17.770
From sweets to cosmetics,

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:19.233
cocoa is everywhere

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:24.170
and 60-70% of it comes
from just two countries,

00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:25.923
Ivory Coast and Ghana.

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:30.930
When you get to farmer households,

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:34.463
you'll find that some may never
even have tasted chocolate.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:38.120
Cocoa farmers largely live

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:42.190
under the poverty line of $1.25 a day.

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:45.633
They really can't even
afford the finished product.

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:51.110
The $100 billion chocolate industry

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:52.640
is plagued by child labor

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:54.670
and has historically been a leading cause

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:58.040
of deforestation in both
West African nations

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:00.473
that receive a pittance
for selling their beans.

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:03.540
The two countries alone represent less

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:05.870
than 6% of the global value,

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:10.100
including the taxes that the
country is taking from exports.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:12.600
The farmer is probably
getting less than 2%.

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:15.610
But soon, even these small percentages

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:17.080
may be lost completely,

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:19.470
as the main importer
of West African beans,

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:22.110
the European Union, gears up to ban cocoa

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:25.293
with a harmful impact on the
environment and human rights.

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:29.030
If there was a legislation there

00:01:29.030 --> 00:01:32.480
that prevented West Africans
from selling their cocoa,

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:34.290
there would really be an issue

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:37.590
for the economies, for the
farmers, for the governments.

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:40.530
And so, that has added to, I think,

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:43.450
what was initially more of a moral issue

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:45.763
and made it very much an economic issue.

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:48.770
And these attempts by developed nations

00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:51.200
to force a cleanup of cocoa supply chain,

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:53.020
while potentially hurting the livelihoods

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:54.490
of West African farmers,

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:56.853
might not even achieve
their desired goals.

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:01.760
Cocoa and chocolate world
are stuck in colonialism,

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:03.370
making the poor nations poorer

00:02:03.370 --> 00:02:05.080
and the rich nations richer.

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:06.680
And that has not changed at all.

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:21.140
Cocoa, a tree crop native

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:22.860
to Central and South America,

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:25.773
was introduced to Africa
over a hundred years ago.

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:30.780
Colonial powers like France
and Britain compelled

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:33.320
some of their colonies in Africa

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:35.870
to cultivate crops that
they don't want to cultivate

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:38.333
because of the ecological costs.

00:02:39.190 --> 00:02:40.710
After independence these countries

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:43.830
just keep on producing this raw material

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:48.620
which they export to Europe and America.

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:51.523
It's almost a classic
colonial division of labor.

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:54.720
Cocoa has remained intertwined

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:57.630
with the economies of
Ghana and Ivory Coast,

00:02:57.630 --> 00:02:59.670
their fortunes often rising and falling

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:01.020
with the price of the crop.

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:04.090
After Ivory Coast, or Côte d'Ivoire,

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:06.490
gained independence in 1960,

00:03:06.490 --> 00:03:08.920
its first president and
the political elites

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:11.633
were cocoa growers who
encouraged production.

00:03:12.710 --> 00:03:15.880
In Côte d'Ivoire, in
the 1950s, 1960s, '70s,

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:19.870
some of the richest men
were actually cocoa farmers.

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:22.230
And there was a speech by
the first president saying

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:25.300
if you want to be rich, plant cocoa.

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:28.000
But the global price of cocoa continued

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:30.020
to reduce in the early '60s

00:03:30.020 --> 00:03:31.890
to the point that in 1965,

00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:34.600
some cocoa planters in Ghana judged

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:38.513
that it makes no sense for
them to harvest their cocoa.

00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:43.240
The first president of
Ghana, Kwame Nkrumah,

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:45.200
had seen his country's economic reliance

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:48.143
on a crop with such
volatile prices as perilous.

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:52.400
He stopped funding new cocoa farms

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:54.690
and then tried to diversify.

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:57.080
Also tried to create a situation

00:03:57.080 --> 00:04:00.180
whereby Ghana would be
processing its cocoa.

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:03.030
Unfortunately, that
didn't come to fruition

00:04:03.030 --> 00:04:06.833
because of the 1966 coup against him.

00:04:08.170 --> 00:04:10.640
The volatility of cocoa prices continued

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:13.400
to destabilize the Ghanaian currency.

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:15.520
And after a second coup in the '80s,

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:18.450
the government of Ghana made
a pledge to its cocoa farmers

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:20.000
to never let the farmgate -

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:22.180
the price it would pay
farmers for their cocoa -

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:24.113
slide below a certain threshold.

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:29.050
So there are two prices.

00:04:29.050 --> 00:04:30.830
One is fixed by the government

00:04:30.830 --> 00:04:33.630
and one is determined by the markets.

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:36.720
The price of cocoa on
the international markets

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:41.150
is essentially affected
by supply and demand,

00:04:41.150 --> 00:04:44.360
with most of the supply
coming from West Africa

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:46.480
and most of the demand coming from

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:49.463
the EU and also North America.

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:52.900
Cocoa is the main export revenue

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:55.583
for Ivory Coast and the
third biggest for Ghana.

00:04:56.490 --> 00:04:58.990
About 60% of this exported cocoa

00:04:58.990 --> 00:05:00.740
ends up in the European Union,

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:03.053
mainly in the form of unprocessed beans.

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:06.383
While we talk about price of cocoa,

00:05:07.370 --> 00:05:09.260
the world is changing.

00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:13.090
Supply and demand is probably
not the market of tomorrow,

00:05:13.090 --> 00:05:17.020
because we have another element,
which is sustainability.

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:19.730
So we see in Europe,
there are even right now

00:05:19.730 --> 00:05:24.613
regulation proposals to ban
cocoa linked to deforestation.

00:05:27.244 --> 00:05:28.180
Cocoa's connection

00:05:28.180 --> 00:05:30.740
to deforestation dates back decades,

00:05:30.740 --> 00:05:32.670
a direct link to the traditional method

00:05:32.670 --> 00:05:35.130
of cultivation used in
Ivory Coast and Ghana

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:36.443
called slash and burn.

00:05:37.610 --> 00:05:39.520
Farmers just cut down these trees

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:41.320
and you burn the entire land.

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:44.650
Then you plant, harvest, and
then move to another forest,

00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:46.390
plant, harvest.

00:05:46.390 --> 00:05:48.410
So after slash and burn planting,

00:05:48.410 --> 00:05:49.987
you tend to harvest quickly

00:05:51.070 --> 00:05:53.870
and you tend to use less labor.

00:05:53.870 --> 00:05:54.844
The problem with this is

00:05:54.844 --> 00:05:59.010
that the amount of forest is finite.

00:05:59.010 --> 00:06:03.950
So in Côte d'Ivoire there's
almost complete deforestation.

00:06:03.950 --> 00:06:08.490
There's no new land for
cocoa farmers to move to.

00:06:08.490 --> 00:06:11.070
To try and reduce this deforestation,

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:13.590
Ivory Coast and Ghana
have been forcing farmers

00:06:13.590 --> 00:06:15.900
to replant cocoa on the same land,

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:19.330
even as the soil's nutrients get depleted.

00:06:19.330 --> 00:06:21.000
But replanting on the same spot

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:24.230
requires more workers and
more input to grow the crop,

00:06:24.230 --> 00:06:27.550
shrinking farmers' already tight margins.

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:29.260
You now have to weed.

00:06:29.260 --> 00:06:32.330
You now have to buy
fertilizers, pesticides.

00:06:32.330 --> 00:06:33.960
Second, third replanting,

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:37.060
you need to increase
your use of fertilizers.

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:39.500
So if your cost of
production keeps going up

00:06:39.500 --> 00:06:41.630
and you have no control over the price,

00:06:41.630 --> 00:06:44.860
then you have to look
for a way of deflating

00:06:44.860 --> 00:06:46.870
these extra costs,

00:06:46.870 --> 00:06:48.060
which is one of the causes

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:51.020
of increases in child labor
and child trafficking.

00:06:51.020 --> 00:06:53.133
This is a way of deflating cost of labor.

00:06:54.330 --> 00:06:56.360
Cocoa is not an easy crop to grow

00:06:56.360 --> 00:06:59.480
and it competes for labor with
other activities in the area,

00:06:59.480 --> 00:07:02.400
like small scale gold mining,
which is often illegal

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:04.473
and known locally in Ghana as galamsey.

00:07:17.450 --> 00:07:21.210
Cocoa farmers often run farms as a family,

00:07:21.210 --> 00:07:24.810
which is why cocoa poverty
is such a big issue

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:27.484
because a farmer would support a family

00:07:27.484 --> 00:07:29.800
of six or eight people.

00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:32.590
Usually it's small-scale farmers.

00:07:32.590 --> 00:07:35.110
So they plant in the farm
and then they come home

00:07:35.110 --> 00:07:37.860
and dry the beans before packaging them

00:07:37.860 --> 00:07:40.520
and selling it to, in Ghana's case,

00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:43.713
the Cocoa Board through licensed buyers.

00:07:45.470 --> 00:07:46.923
The problem with this is

00:07:46.923 --> 00:07:49.100
that most of the benefits,

00:07:49.100 --> 00:07:51.390
it's mainly in the
manufactured aspect of it,

00:07:51.390 --> 00:07:55.510
while most of the costs - for
example, ecological costs -

00:07:55.510 --> 00:07:57.853
is mainly in the raw
material aspect of it.

00:08:00.140 --> 00:08:01.790
Most cocoa beans are processed

00:08:01.790 --> 00:08:03.930
once they reach their final destination

00:08:03.930 --> 00:08:06.323
in the factories of
chocolate-making companies.

00:08:07.310 --> 00:08:10.180
A study of the chocolate
value chain by Oxfam showed

00:08:10.180 --> 00:08:12.550
that the grinding and
production of chocolate amounts

00:08:12.550 --> 00:08:15.900
to more than 25% of the price of a bar.

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:18.950
While in the '70s, when cocoa
prices hit record highs,

00:08:18.950 --> 00:08:21.270
the farmers' share was nearly 50%,

00:08:21.270 --> 00:08:23.550
today it's down to around 6%,

00:08:23.550 --> 00:08:27.190
and that's likely to include
taxes and fees of middlemen.

00:08:27.190 --> 00:08:28.580
Today there's more middlemen.

00:08:28.580 --> 00:08:30.870
They are basically
squeezing out the profit

00:08:30.870 --> 00:08:32.150
for cocoa farmers.

00:08:32.150 --> 00:08:34.340
That's one way of explaining it.

00:08:34.340 --> 00:08:38.120
Another way would be to say
that there's more consolidation

00:08:38.120 --> 00:08:41.650
of market power in the
chocolate production,

00:08:41.650 --> 00:08:45.040
as well as the retail
part of the value chain.

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:46.710
One other way of doing it

00:08:47.620 --> 00:08:49.830
is to look at the cost of production.

00:08:49.830 --> 00:08:51.547
So the cost of producing cocoa now

00:08:51.547 --> 00:08:56.547
and the cost of producing
cocoa in the 1960s and 1970s,

00:08:56.970 --> 00:08:58.783
it's not even close.

00:09:03.170 --> 00:09:06.090
A new cocoa is demanded in the market.

00:09:06.090 --> 00:09:08.360
Cocoa produced sustainably.

00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:12.060
And we need to ensure that anyone

00:09:12.060 --> 00:09:14.830
that feels that sustainability
is just about deforestation,

00:09:14.830 --> 00:09:16.060
it's not the case.

00:09:16.060 --> 00:09:20.170
Deforestation or any human rights abuse

00:09:20.170 --> 00:09:25.080
seen in the cocoa sector are
the consequence of poverty.

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:27.263
And we need to tackle the root cause.

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:31.560
To tackle farmer poverty,

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:33.430
the government of Ivory Coast and Ghana

00:09:33.430 --> 00:09:35.970
introduced an initiative in 2019

00:09:35.970 --> 00:09:39.140
called a Living Income
Differential, or LID,

00:09:39.140 --> 00:09:42.140
which requires confectionary
companies to pay a premium

00:09:42.140 --> 00:09:45.613
of 400 US dollars on each
tonne of cocoa they buy.

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:50.420
This money is then incorporated
into the farmgate price

00:09:50.420 --> 00:09:53.290
to bring a bigger slice
of the bar to the farmer.

00:09:53.290 --> 00:09:55.030
In the first year of the LID,

00:09:55.030 --> 00:09:59.260
the farmgate price in both
countries increased nearly 20%.

00:09:59.260 --> 00:10:00.980
The World Cocoa Foundation

00:10:00.980 --> 00:10:03.790
that brings together
companies representing 80%

00:10:03.790 --> 00:10:07.544
of the global cocoa market
has welcomed the change.

00:10:07.544 --> 00:10:10.100
This new tool is really effective

00:10:10.100 --> 00:10:12.580
in getting farmers out of poverty.

00:10:12.580 --> 00:10:16.460
We think that it is
not possible to sustain

00:10:16.460 --> 00:10:20.070
the cocoa industry at the
detriment of the farmers.

00:10:20.070 --> 00:10:23.200
So it's not a nice thing to do, actually,

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:24.800
it is the right thing to do

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:27.410
if we want to sustain the cocoa industry

00:10:27.410 --> 00:10:30.373
for the benefits of all the
members of the supply chain.

00:10:45.004 --> 00:10:46.960
If you're a cocoa farmer,

00:10:46.960 --> 00:10:49.120
your income increases by 20%.

00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:53.040
You are now happy, and yes,
there's more money in cocoa.

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:55.180
So if you are thinking of leaving cocoa,

00:10:55.180 --> 00:10:56.700
there's no point leaving now.

00:10:56.700 --> 00:10:58.910
The inevitable outcome of this would be

00:10:58.910 --> 00:11:01.080
an increase in outputs.

00:11:01.080 --> 00:11:03.020
So an increase in outputs would mean

00:11:03.020 --> 00:11:05.590
a further reduction in market price.

00:11:05.590 --> 00:11:07.610
And in fact, in the last cocoa season,

00:11:07.610 --> 00:11:10.870
within Ghana's case, it had
a record production actually.

00:11:10.870 --> 00:11:13.500
So there was actually oversupply.

00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:17.240
And so, that also gave
less negotiation room

00:11:17.240 --> 00:11:22.240
in terms of fixing pricing
with their main buyers.

00:11:22.660 --> 00:11:25.940
So this is actually good for cocoa buyers,

00:11:25.940 --> 00:11:28.720
for multinationals who want more cocoa.

00:11:28.720 --> 00:11:29.750
But I think it will be bad

00:11:29.750 --> 00:11:31.950
for Côte d'Ivoire and
Ghana in the long run.

00:11:33.830 --> 00:11:35.720
In an effort to clean up the supply chain,

00:11:35.720 --> 00:11:38.270
governments have also been
trying to create mechanisms

00:11:38.270 --> 00:11:40.110
to ensure the traceability of beans

00:11:40.110 --> 00:11:42.169
to the farm where they were grown.

00:11:42.169 --> 00:11:44.490
Ultimately, that would be the first step.

00:11:44.490 --> 00:11:48.190
If we can trace the cocoa,
that would help make

00:11:48.190 --> 00:11:50.410
for a cleaner cocoa industry

00:11:50.410 --> 00:11:52.480
and cleaner cocoa supply chain

00:11:52.480 --> 00:11:55.600
where all the stakeholders
along that supply chain

00:11:55.600 --> 00:11:57.870
can be held responsible.

00:11:57.870 --> 00:12:01.750
So that will be really the quickest fix,

00:12:01.750 --> 00:12:04.300
to put it that way, even
though it's taken a while,

00:12:04.300 --> 00:12:06.210
but the bigger opportunity really

00:12:06.210 --> 00:12:10.900
is to improve industrialization
and manufacturing.

00:12:10.900 --> 00:12:12.482
That would be a way of breaking the

00:12:12.482 --> 00:12:14.170
neo-colonial patterns of trade.

00:12:14.170 --> 00:12:16.510
So to break that chain would be

00:12:16.510 --> 00:12:21.253
to process the raw materials
in Africa before you export.

00:12:22.890 --> 00:12:24.440
African economies have been locked

00:12:24.440 --> 00:12:27.140
in similar trading patterns
with other commodities,

00:12:27.140 --> 00:12:29.070
like crude oil or cotton;

00:12:29.070 --> 00:12:31.350
exporting raw materials for a low cost

00:12:31.350 --> 00:12:33.700
and then importing finished goods.

00:12:33.700 --> 00:12:35.230
But a growing taste for chocolate

00:12:35.230 --> 00:12:38.000
and a rising middle class
provides an opportunity

00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:41.360
to keep more of the value
of cocoa within Africa.

00:12:41.360 --> 00:12:43.500
And so, in Ghana's capital, Accra,

00:12:43.500 --> 00:12:46.703
an industry of artisanal
chocolate makers has taken root.

00:12:48.030 --> 00:12:49.100
It's a work of passion.

00:12:49.100 --> 00:12:52.053
I've fallen so much in love
with cocoa and chocolate.

00:12:52.980 --> 00:12:56.420
When I started, it was very
difficult to get information

00:12:56.420 --> 00:13:00.500
on cocoa processing and
chocolate production.

00:13:00.500 --> 00:13:04.263
I had to go to the U.K. to
learn how to use the chocolate.

00:13:05.140 --> 00:13:07.320
However, in the last six years,

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:10.850
there's a growing small industry.

00:13:10.850 --> 00:13:11.900
I think it's important

00:13:11.900 --> 00:13:13.750
that now the current administration

00:13:13.750 --> 00:13:18.400
is pushing the eating of
chocolate and cocoa products.

00:13:18.400 --> 00:13:20.910
Bioko Treats is a boutique chocolate brand

00:13:20.910 --> 00:13:23.620
that caters to emerging local appetites,

00:13:23.620 --> 00:13:26.080
but it hasn't always been a smooth ride.

00:13:26.080 --> 00:13:28.560
Access to affordable funding,

00:13:28.560 --> 00:13:31.460
you know, it's just about non-existent.

00:13:31.460 --> 00:13:35.970
So the company cannot grow
maybe as quickly as it should.

00:13:35.970 --> 00:13:36.860
To have an impact

00:13:36.860 --> 00:13:38.540
on the wider chocolate industry,

00:13:38.540 --> 00:13:41.003
change needs to happen on a larger scale.

00:13:41.920 --> 00:13:43.420
Hendrik Reimers is the founder

00:13:43.420 --> 00:13:46.430
of Ghana-based chocolate maker, Fairafric.

00:13:46.430 --> 00:13:47.900
With no bank willing to fund

00:13:47.900 --> 00:13:50.040
what was perceived as a risky project,

00:13:50.040 --> 00:13:52.793
his company went online
to crowdfund a factory.

00:13:53.680 --> 00:13:55.640
It was built in less than half a year

00:13:55.640 --> 00:13:58.270
and has the capacity to
produce 4 million bars

00:13:58.270 --> 00:13:59.990
of chocolate a month.

00:13:59.990 --> 00:14:02.910
Currently, the advantages
of producing chocolate

00:14:02.910 --> 00:14:06.890
in West Africa are not all that many,

00:14:06.890 --> 00:14:09.440
rather to the contrary, we do face a lot

00:14:09.440 --> 00:14:13.440
of infrastructure challenges.
Where we are producing here

00:14:13.440 --> 00:14:15.140
we had to bring our own internet,

00:14:15.140 --> 00:14:17.260
we have to supply our own water,

00:14:17.260 --> 00:14:19.720
own electricity most of the time,

00:14:19.720 --> 00:14:23.190
we depend on our own
wastewater management.

00:14:23.190 --> 00:14:24.730
So technically speaking,

00:14:24.730 --> 00:14:28.040
it will be much easier to
produce chocolate in Europe

00:14:28.040 --> 00:14:30.600
where all that infrastructure
is already set up.

00:14:30.600 --> 00:14:33.350
The choice of producing in rural Ghana

00:14:33.350 --> 00:14:36.010
is one to make a statement
and to show the world

00:14:36.010 --> 00:14:38.870
that we can make world
class chocolate here

00:14:38.870 --> 00:14:40.370
where the cocoa grows as well.

00:14:42.307 --> 00:14:44.980
Fairafric is the only large-scale factory

00:14:44.980 --> 00:14:47.600
in Ghana producing chocolate for export,

00:14:47.600 --> 00:14:50.810
but the company's ambition is
to grow the domestic industry

00:14:50.810 --> 00:14:54.030
so it can compete in the
international market.

00:14:54.030 --> 00:14:56.110
We set up a chocolaterie school

00:14:56.110 --> 00:14:58.100
where we train local talents

00:14:58.100 --> 00:15:01.123
in the art of praline making.

00:15:01.960 --> 00:15:04.530
We exported the first
shipment of pralines,

00:15:04.530 --> 00:15:07.610
which the students have made
after a year of training.

00:15:07.610 --> 00:15:09.870
And one of the trainers said

00:15:09.870 --> 00:15:13.440
this is the same quality
that we have made in Belgium.

00:15:13.440 --> 00:15:16.460
So that was one of the proudest
moments I've ever seen,

00:15:16.460 --> 00:15:18.309
you know, people seeing
that they're getting

00:15:18.309 --> 00:15:20.620
that kind of comparison, having made

00:15:20.620 --> 00:15:23.070
like the same quality that is produced

00:15:23.070 --> 00:15:26.420
in Switzerland and in
Belgium here in rural Ghana.

00:15:26.420 --> 00:15:29.193
I think that's a significant game-changer.

00:15:31.160 --> 00:15:33.010
The company aims to source everything

00:15:33.010 --> 00:15:35.040
within the African continent.

00:15:35.040 --> 00:15:37.910
And the reasonably long
shelf-life of commercial chocolate

00:15:37.910 --> 00:15:41.770
has allowed Fairafric to
overcome supply chain issues.

00:15:41.770 --> 00:15:43.910
But despite the appeal of this model,

00:15:43.910 --> 00:15:46.660
none of the multinational
companies have followed suit.

00:15:47.650 --> 00:15:50.300
The real reason that we see very,

00:15:50.300 --> 00:15:51.970
very little infrastructure

00:15:51.970 --> 00:15:55.330
in terms of adding value to resources

00:15:55.330 --> 00:15:58.610
are the tariff kind of policies of Europe

00:15:58.610 --> 00:16:01.470
that have always said you
can bring in your commodities

00:16:01.470 --> 00:16:03.210
without any duties paid,

00:16:03.210 --> 00:16:04.770
but if you've made something out of it -

00:16:04.770 --> 00:16:06.280
be it semi-finished products,

00:16:06.280 --> 00:16:08.830
like cocoa mass or cocoa butter,

00:16:08.830 --> 00:16:11.020
or even chocolate finished products -

00:16:11.020 --> 00:16:12.890
then duties have applied.

00:16:12.890 --> 00:16:16.330
So you've immediately been
at a cost disadvantage

00:16:16.330 --> 00:16:18.680
and it forced all the value addition

00:16:18.680 --> 00:16:21.970
to commodities towards or into Europe.

00:16:21.970 --> 00:16:24.290
And only since around like 20 years,

00:16:24.290 --> 00:16:25.300
that has been changed

00:16:25.300 --> 00:16:28.060
and the playing field was leveled.

00:16:28.060 --> 00:16:32.860
If you consider this very
issue of local processing,

00:16:32.860 --> 00:16:35.650
it is reflective of the set of regulation

00:16:35.650 --> 00:16:37.970
that has been put in place.

00:16:37.970 --> 00:16:39.810
Depending on the way the government

00:16:39.810 --> 00:16:42.130
is adjusting the regulation,

00:16:42.130 --> 00:16:45.630
maybe tomorrow's picture
would be different.

00:16:45.630 --> 00:16:47.590
Government, for example, is pushing

00:16:47.590 --> 00:16:50.440
to having national investors in it,

00:16:50.440 --> 00:16:53.240
but is considering to reach out first

00:16:53.240 --> 00:16:55.060
to the private sector

00:16:55.060 --> 00:16:57.303
and try to reach the win-win agreement.

00:16:58.820 --> 00:17:01.660
I think politically in the spirit

00:17:01.660 --> 00:17:05.730
of African self-sufficiency,
Africa rising,

00:17:05.730 --> 00:17:07.820
the idea is one that anyone

00:17:07.820 --> 00:17:09.700
can really easily embrace,

00:17:09.700 --> 00:17:13.100
but the practice of it is one
that's difficult to implement.

00:17:13.100 --> 00:17:15.380
The more components there
are in your products,

00:17:15.380 --> 00:17:17.370
the more standards you have to meet.

00:17:17.370 --> 00:17:20.020
The fact that you can
just export raw beans

00:17:20.020 --> 00:17:21.370
and get your dollars,

00:17:21.370 --> 00:17:24.120
the quickness and ease
with which you can do that,

00:17:24.120 --> 00:17:27.160
is really hard to overlook.

00:17:27.160 --> 00:17:29.750
Because of the export revenue component,

00:17:29.750 --> 00:17:33.180
it means that there's always
going to be a trade-off.

00:17:33.180 --> 00:17:35.390
It's not just a matter of
transforming the beans,

00:17:35.390 --> 00:17:38.190
but getting it to a point
where you can export and earn.

00:17:39.040 --> 00:17:41.050
And alongside economic incentives

00:17:41.050 --> 00:17:43.540
and regulations, the role of consumers

00:17:43.540 --> 00:17:44.930
in shifting the whole industry

00:17:44.930 --> 00:17:47.443
shouldn't be underestimated,
as history shows.

00:17:48.390 --> 00:17:50.480
Consumers, as far back as a hundred years,

00:17:50.480 --> 00:17:53.900
have forced Cadbury to
source their cocoa from Ghana

00:17:53.900 --> 00:17:56.200
where slavery was already abolished.

00:17:56.200 --> 00:17:58.890
The consumer is not used to the idea

00:17:58.890 --> 00:18:01.720
of 'Made in Africa' yet,
or 'Made in 'Ghana'.

00:18:01.720 --> 00:18:03.380
If you tell someone 'Made in Germany',

00:18:03.380 --> 00:18:05.120
that really rings a bell, that resonates

00:18:05.120 --> 00:18:06.780
and it has a certain message.

00:18:06.780 --> 00:18:11.080
And although it kind
of looks simple to say,

00:18:11.080 --> 00:18:12.350
look, we are adding a lot of value,

00:18:12.350 --> 00:18:14.910
we create meaningful, well-paid jobs

00:18:14.910 --> 00:18:16.770
producing chocolate here,

00:18:16.770 --> 00:18:18.650
you only have that split second

00:18:18.650 --> 00:18:21.193
when a consumer decides to buy chocolate.

00:18:21.193 --> 00:18:22.600
It's been a major challenge,

00:18:22.600 --> 00:18:25.853
but we've done a lot in
terms of explaining that.

00:18:28.140 --> 00:18:31.030
Almost everyone loves chocolate,

00:18:31.030 --> 00:18:34.440
but it's important to
ensure that it's cleaner,

00:18:34.440 --> 00:18:35.940
less heavy on the environment,

00:18:35.940 --> 00:18:39.923
but also fairer on the people
that actually toil the land.

